# Line endings and binary handling.
#
# Without this file git falls back to each machine's `core.autocrlf`, which on
# Windows is `true` — every checkout rewrites LF to CRLF and every `git add`
# prints a wall of "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings. Worse, files can
# show as modified with an empty diff, which is noise that hides real changes.
#
# So the repository decides instead of the machine: LF in the object store and
# LF in the working tree, everywhere. Nothing in this project cares — Python,
# Node, Metro and every editor in use handle LF on Windows — and it makes a
# checkout byte-identical across platforms.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries. Git's own detection is good, but being explicit means a heuristic
# can never decide that a sprite sheet is text and corrupt it by "normalising"
# the bytes that happen to look like line endings.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary

*.wav   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.m4a   binary

*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks   binary

# SVG is text and wants to stay diffable — the art pipeline regenerates these,
# and a readable diff is how you tell a re-export from a real change.
*.svg   text

# Windows batch files are parsed by cmd.exe, which needs CRLF. Nothing here
# tracks one today (`android/` is ignored), but a later `expo prebuild` would
# bring `gradlew.bat` in, and a LF copy of it fails in ways that are hard to
# read.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
